  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nTODAY AND TOMORROW...Early morning temperatures are comfortable \n(perhaps even a little crisp!) as current observations feature low \nto mid 50s outside of the Twin Cities heat island. There are even a \ncouple of reports in the upper 40s at this hour across central MN. \nAnother pleasant day of weather is expected, but it will be slightly\nwarmer with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s. An area of high pressure\nover southern MN & northern IA will be the main influence on our\nweather, supporting light winds and mainly clear skies outside \nof a few daytime cumulus clouds. High pressure remains in place \nfor Thursday, however temperatures are forecast to warm back to \nnear normal values in the low to mid 80s due to a southerly wind\nshift associated with a shortwave moving across central Canada.\n\nNEXT RAIN CHANCES FRIDAY INTO THE WEEKEND...The large scale view \ncaptures an upper-low over Hudson Bay and a ridge over the desert \nsouthwest heading into this weekend. The Upper Midwest will remain \npositioned in between these two features, within a region of \nnorthwesterly flow aloft. A series of shortwaves are forecast to \nmove across central Canada and will send a few fronts south\nacross the region Friday-Monday. Latest model suite brings the \nfirst front through the forecast area Friday afternoon-evening.\nScattered showers and a few storms may develop ahead of the \nfrontal passage, however severe weather is currently not expected.\nThe best chance for storms will be across southeastern MN and \nwestern WI during the evening. The weekend high temperature\nforecast features some uncertainty due to the depth of cooler \nair aloft captured in model guidance. Simply put, there is a \nspread in how far south the coolest air will get following the \nfrontal passage on Friday. For now, the NBM's highs in the low \nto the mid-80s are a reasonable forecast, but further refinements\nup or down may be needed over the coming days. In addition to \nhaving an impact on the temperature forecast, the remnant \nbaroclinic zone will act as the tracks for addition shower and \nstorm chances through the weekend. The best chance for \nprecipitation after Friday evening will be Sunday evening into \nMonday, as a potentially stronger wave moves across the Upper \nMidwest. Temperatures take a step back into the lower 80s \nfollowing the precipitation chances early next week.",
